{"archetypes":[{"name":"harness-evidence","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The test plan names specific helpers and env vars (write_stub_dispatch, AGGREGATE_DISPATCH_SH, AGGREGATE_STUB_MODE, AGGREGATE_STUB_MERGE_KIND, TMP, AGG) as pre-existing patterns; if any name is wrong or the helper signature differs, both new tests silently fail or never reach the intended code path.","prompt_body":"Read `skills/review/scripts/test-aggregate-findings.sh` in full and verify that every helper function name, environment variable name, and binding (write_stub_dispatch, AGGREGATE_DISPATCH_SH, AGGREGATE_STUB_MODE, AGGREGATE_STUB_MERGE_KIND, TMP, AGG) the plan references as pre-existing actually appears in the file with those exact spellings and the call signatures the plan assumes. Check that the stub-dispatch pattern the plan proposes for Test 2 (setting env vars before invocation, reading an env-capture output file, asserting AGGREGATED and REASON keys) is consistent with how existing stub tests use those same env vars. If any name is absent or the pattern differs, the new tests would run the wrong code path or fail with an uninformative error rather than testing the new flag. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"flag-grammar-parity","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan claims the new flag uses the same true|false grammar and parse shape as --codex-present and --cursor-present, but the proposed case arm uses shift 2 and ${2:?} while the existing boolean flags may use shift 1 or a different positional pattern, and the validation block format needs to match exactly to keep the argv loop coherent.","prompt_body":"Read `skills/review/scripts/aggregate-findings.sh` and compare the actual parse-case arms and post-loop validation blocks for `--codex-present` and `--cursor-present` against the plan's proposed `--allow-findings-outside-tmpdir) ALLOW_FINDINGS_OUTSIDE_TMPDIR=\"${2:?}\"; shift 2` pattern. Check whether existing boolean flags use `shift 1` or `shift 2`, whether they consume the next positional with `$2` or `$1`, and whether their validation error messages use the same exit code, function name, and prose grammar the plan prescribes. A mismatch in shift count would silently consume an unrelated subsequent argument; a mismatch in validation format would break style consistency. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"containment-asymmetry","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan's core security claim is that only the rejection branch of the containment case is flag-gated while the symlink rejection and the post-dispatch _cand_canon check remain unconditionally strict; this needs to be verified against the actual code layout to confirm no execution path allows both input and output to escape --review-tmpdir simultaneously.","prompt_body":"Read `skills/review/scripts/aggregate-findings.sh` and map the exact code sections that perform (a) symlink rejection, (b) `_findings_canon` canonicalization and containment case, and (c) `_cand_canon` post-dispatch resolves-under-tmpdir check. Verify that sections (a) and (c) have no conditional branch that reads `ALLOW_FINDINGS_OUTSIDE_TMPDIR` today, and confirm the plan's proposed gate wraps only the rejection arm of (b). Also check whether there is any scenario where `--findings-file` is the same path as a dispatch artifact (`aggregator-prompt.md`, LLM candidate output) — in that case the `mv -f` rewrite in the success path would land outside `--review-tmpdir` and the `_cand_canon` check might not catch it if the candidate temp file is resolved before the move. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
